                "2. Reliability-Centered Maintenance (RCM) Paradigm: Developed by F. Stanley Nowlan and Howard F. Heap in the commercial aviation industry, RCM establishes a structured methodology for determining the optimum maintenance strategy for physical assets based on operational consequences and safety criticality. Nowlan and Heap demonstrated that the classical belief in time-based wear-out (the assumption that failure probability strictly correlates with equipment age) applies to only eleven percent of modern components, while eighty-nine percent of failure modes exhibit random, infant mortality, or stress-induced patterns. In residential hall facilities, plumbing leaks, electrical switch failures, and lock jams occur randomly as direct functions of usage intensity rather than predictable chronological age. RCM dictates that maintenance information systems must prioritize rapid Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) and rapid Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA) over rigid periodic schedules. HostelFix embeds RCM logic by classifying maintenance reports across four objective severity tiers (Urgent, High, Medium, Low), ensuring that high-consequence safety hazards bypass bureaucratic queues for rapid triage.",
